Ok, let's do this the right way. I happen to have, on a shelf behind me,
a copy of the 1999 ISO C Standard. (Nothing to brag about, btw; if you
ask your local bookshop (Support your local bookshop! Don't shop over
the 'net if you can avoid it! If you don't support your local bookshop,
you will lose it, and deservedly) to order ISBN 0-470-84573-2 for you,
you can have one too, for (AFAICT) only USD 65 or GBP 34.95. No serious
C programmer can afford to be without it (though in a pinch, the last
public draft from <http://std.dkuug.dk/JTC1/SC22/WG14/> will serve).) 

> Do you mean that the order of processing arguments isn't defined by ansi C?

That's just the least of your worries. But no, order of argument
processing is not defined by ISO C (it's been ISO C for well over a
decade now, BTW; ANSI Standards don't apply to those of us in the free
world), or by most other languages, in fact. Only languages which aren't
expected to provide decent performance can afford to demand a specific
order, not necessarily suitable for the architecture the program runs
on. In fact, there's not guaranteed to _be_ an order at all.

Ok, but I promised quotes from the Standard. what does it say about all
this? First of all, in paragraph 6.5, item 2, we have:
# Between the previous and the next sequence point an object shall have
# its stored value modified at most once by the evaluation of an
# expression. Furthermore, the prior value shall be read only to
# determine the value to be stored.
We can agree, I think, that the first ++x modifies the value of x, and
that the second argument reads the value of that same x for other
purposes than to determine the value the first argument should store.
So, _if_ this occurs between two adjacent sequence points, that is in
violation of this paragraph.
But what is a sequence point? Ah, well... part of 5.1.2.3#2 says:
# At certain specified points in the execution sequence called /sequence
# points/...
but does not define where these sequence points are. They are, in fact,
defined throughout the text, where appropriate. A summary of sequence
points is in annex C; suffice it to say that there are none between two
arguments in a function call, though there _is_ one just after the
evaluation of all arguments and before the function is called. In fact,
6.5.2.2#10 says
# The order of evaluation of the function designator, the actual
# arguments, and subexpressions within the actual arguments is
# unspecified, but there is a sequence point before the actual call.

So, alas, your printf() call is in violation of 6.5#2. But what is the
effect of this? Paragraph 4, item 2, starts out:
# If a "shall" or "shall not" requirement that appears outside of a
# constraint is violated, the behaviour is undefined.
And since 6.5#2 is, in fact, outside any constraints, this violation
does cause undefined behaviour. And undefined behaviour? 3.4.3#1:
# _undefined behaviour_
# behaviour, upon use of a nonportable or erroneous program construct or
# of erroneous data, for which this International Standard imposes no
# requirements
Note: _no_ requirements. Upon execution of the function call
printf("%d%d\n", ++x, ++x), your program can do _anything_, as far as
the ISO C Standard is concerned. Obviously, it's not going to cause
global thermonuclear war (unless your computer is in the Pentagon, but
even then I'd be more wary of your superiors...), but it might, just
might, format your hard drive... Don't look surprised, some kinds of UB
can cause malicious code to be executed. For a notorious example, search
the Web for the Great Internet Worm; for a more recent but painfully
similar example, look at Sasser, and shake your head in amazement that
MicroSoft have _still_ not got their act together. Bloody amateurs.

Oh, and here's an example why that code could cause a crash in a
realistic, modern-day scenario. Suppose a modern CPU, with several (at
least two) code pipelines, and a compiler which takes advantage of them.
To call a function with two arguments, it would shove code for fetching
and evaluating the first argument in pipeline A, and for the second
argument in pipeline B. Wait for them to finish - this should take about
half as long as on a non-pipelined machine, which is why we do it this
way. Then, call the function using the result from A and the result from
B. Normally, no problem. However... three scenarios:

Scenario One:
Initialisation of pipelines can be done simultaneously.
Tick 1: Initialise code for A; initialise code for B. A and B idle.
Tick 2: A fetches x; B fetches x. Simultaneously. Need not be a problem.
Tick 3: A increases the value it got; B increases the value it got. OK.
Tick 4: A writes this value to x; B writes its value to x.
        Simultaneously. The memory controller goes "Whoa! Memory Access
        Conflict!" and halts the program.

Scenario Two:
Initialisation of pipelines can not be done simultaneously.
Tick 1: Initialise code for A. A and B idle.
Tick 2: Initialise code for B. A fetches x. B idle.
Tick 3: A increases the value it got. B fetches x - which still has the
        old value.
Tick 4: A writes its new value to x. B increases the _old_ value it has.
Tick 5: A has finished, and is waiting. B writes its new value to x.
No clashes in this scenario, but you end up with x effectively being
increased _once_, and two 2's being printed.

Scenario Three:
Initialisation of pipelines can not be done simultaneously, but
increment-and-write is a single tick.
Tick 1: Initialise code for A. A and B idle.
Tick 2: Initialise code for B. A fetches x. B idle.
Tick 3: A increases the value it got, and writes it to x. B fetches x -
        while A is writing it! Even if this does not cause a trap, it
        could easily cause B to fetch part of the old, and part of the
        new value. For example, instead of 00000001, or 00000010, it
        could get the first seven bits before, and the last bit after A
        has written it. The value B fetches would then be 0...

None of this is particularly eccentric. It may not be how it happens on
your Wintel box, using the default optimisation settings of your
compiler, but try it on a Sparc, or with optimisations turned up to
eleven...

